<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Magas of Cyrene</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 250 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Magas was ruler of Cyrene, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a>'s queen Berenice I by an earlier marriage. Made governor of Cyrene around 300 BCE, he later declared himself king and in the 270s BCE, allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>, marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> against his half-brother Ptolemy II, until a Libyan revolt forced him back. He ruled Cyrene for about 50 years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/magas-of-cyrene.jpg" alt="Magas of Cyrene" width="320" height="152" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Magas_as_king_of_Kyrene,_circa_282_or_275_to_261_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Magas was the first-born son of the Macedonian noblewoman Berenice and her first husband Philip, an officer in the campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> who commanded one division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a>. He had two younger sisters, Antigone of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and Theoxena of Syracuse. Philip died of natural causes about 318 BCE, and Berenice took her children to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a>, to the entourage of her cousin Eurydice, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a>. By 317 BCE Ptolemy had repudiated Eurydice to marry Berenice, so that Magas became a stepson of Ptolemy I and a member of the Ptolemaic dynasty. His mother bore Ptolemy three children, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsinoe-ii">Arsinoe II</a>, Philotera and the future Ptolemy II.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agathocles-of-syracuse">Agathocles of Syracuse</a> had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ophellas">Ophellas</a>, the governor of Cyrene, put to death in November 308 BCE, Ptolemy ruled Cyrene directly, but the Cyrenaeans rebelled soon after he declared himself king. Around 300 BCE Ptolemy sent Magas, then about twenty, to reconquer the city, and he stayed on as governor. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a>'s death in 283 BCE, Magas tried to win independence from his half-brother Ptolemy II and crowned himself king; the date of his coronation is disputed, with proposals between 283 and 280 BCE and a later date of 276 or 274 BCE.</p><p>He married Apama II, a daughter of the Seleucid king Antiochus I Soter, and Antiochus used the alliance to plan an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Magas attacked Egypt from the west while Antiochus attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, but Magas had to cancel his operations because the nomadic Libyan Marmaridae revolted, and Antiochus was defeated by Ptolemy II.</p><p>Afterwards his reign was largely peaceful and he favoured the arts and sciences, and the Cyrenaic philosophical school. In a treaty of the 260s with the Cretan League of the Oreioi he is called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basileus">basileus</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> is called an eparchia, a province. After 259 BCE he betrothed his only child, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berenice-ii">Berenice II</a>, to Ptolemy II's son, the future Ptolemy III.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Magas died in 250 BCE. Agatharchides, as reported by Athenaeus, records that he spent his rule in luxury and avoided war, and grew so corpulent that he choked on his own fat. His widow Apama II broke the engagement and offered her daughter and the throne to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-the-fair">Demetrius the Fair</a>, son of Demetrius I Poliorcetes, who became king of Cyrene; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berenice-ii">Berenice II</a> had Demetrius killed and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to marry Ptolemy III. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edicts-of-ashoka">Edicts of Ashoka</a> name Magas among the Hellenistic kings to whom the Indian emperor's Buddhist teaching reached, although no Western source records such envoys.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Magas of Cyrene” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ashoka's thirteenth rock edict names the Hellenistic kings Antiochus II, Ptolemy II, Antigonus Gonatas, <b>Magas of Cyrene</b> and Alexander of Epirus as recipients of his dhamma missions, which shows the empire's dealings with the wider world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Magas</b>, son of Ptolemy I's queen Berenice I by an earlier marriage, became governor of Cyrene around 300 BCE after a revolt there was suppressed.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cyrene-under-magas">Kingdom of Cyrene under Magas</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Macedonia. Died in Cyrene, in what is now <a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.82,21.85&amp;year=-260" aria-label="Cyrene on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5110 971 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Magas of Cyrene"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5560 1241h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5560 1241h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=32.82,21.85&amp;year=-260">What was here in 260 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
